{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>Then passed by men, Midianites, etc.</b> One needs to know why the passing of Midianite men is mentioned, since the sale was only to the Ishmaelites, as it says, ״וַיִּמְכְּרוּ אֶת יוֹסֵף לַיִּשְׁמְעֵאלִים״. And this is likewise demonstrated from the verses (Genesis 37:25): ״וְהִנֵּה אֹרְחַת יִשְׁמְעֵאלִים etc. … לְכוּ וְנִמְכְּרֶנּוּ לַיִּשְׁמְעֵאלִים״. If so, the narrative of ״וַיַּעַבְרוּ אֲנָשִׁים מִדְיָנִים״ — its intention is not known." "<b>Further,</b> I see that Scripture additionally says after this (Genesis 37:36): ״וְהַמְּדָנִים מָכְרוּ אוֹתוֹ״ etc.; if so, it is demonstrated that the Midianites sold him, and the first verse contradicts this. Further, Scripture then says (Genesis 39:1): ״וְיוֹסֵף הוּרַד מִצְרָיְמָה וַיִּקְנֵהוּ פּוֹטִיפַר מִיַּד הַיִּשְׁמְעֵאלִים״ — behold, it establishes the sale as having been to the Ishmaelites, as stated at the beginning of the episode, and not to the Midianites. And our Sages of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 84:22) said that this teaches he was sold many times, yet the plain senses of the verses do not reconcile on this matter. Understand this:" "<b>And the correct interpretation</b> is that the explanation of the matter is as follows. For at first Scripture informed us: ״וְהִנֵּה אֹרְחַת יִשְׁמְעֵאלִים״ etc. (Genesis 37:25), and Judah's words when he said, ״לְכוּ וְנִמְכְּרֶנּוּ לַיִּשְׁמְעֵאלִים״. Now since this caravan of Ishmaelites were not traders to deal in every kind of merchandise other than what they were carrying, there was no possibility that they would purchase Joseph. Therefore HaShem provided Midianite men, traders — meaning, those who deal in every kind of merchandise that presents itself, to purchase it for profit. And you should know that the trader is not the one with the purse but the one with the knowledge and expertise, and the one with the purse purchases on his say-so — as is well known in the great commercial centers of the world. And through the mediation of these traders the matter of the sale to the Ishmaelites was completed." "And this is what Scripture means when it says <b>then passed by men, Midianite traders</b> — and through their mediation they sold Joseph to the Ishmaelites, for without them the Ishmaelites would not have purchased him. Now, since the sale involved both parties — the Midianites and the Ishmaelites — the Midianites have a share in the profit inasmuch as they are the buyers, and the Ishmaelites have a share in the profit inasmuch as they are the financiers who put up the twenty silver pieces. Therefore it says <b>and the Midianites sold him to Egypt</b> (Genesis 37:36) etc. — for since they had a share in it and they were also the ones who knew the valuation, they were the sellers, not the Ishmaelites, who are not experts in his worth. But Joseph's person was in the hand of the Ishmaelites, for they were the principals who invested their money in him, and therefore it says <b>and Potiphar purchased him from the hand of the Ishmaelites</b> (Genesis 39:1). Scripture is precise in saying <b>from the hand of</b>, for he was in their hand, since they had brought him down there, and there was no possibility for Potiphar to purchase him other than from the one in whose hand he was. Also, the Ishmaelites could not sell him on their own without the Midianites — apart from the reason that the Midianites were the experts in his valuation and knew how much he was worth, also because they had a share in the profit their consent to the sale was required. Therefore Scripture says that the Midianites sold and took [payment] from the hand of the Ishmaelites. With this the verses are reconciled perfectly, like a table set with all its flowers. And the words of our Sages of blessed memory who said he was sold many times — that is the way of derash. And perhaps, since there were many parties involved in his acquisition, it is reckoned as many sales. And the plain sense of Scripture is as I have written, and the derash shall be expounded." "<b>And they sold Joseph.</b> Perhaps, after they had degraded him so utterly and humiliated him by stripping him naked and casting him into the pit, their anger had subsided somewhat and they were reconciled to Judah's counsel. And corresponding to what they said (Genesis 37:20): ״וְנִרְאֶה מַה יִּהְיוּ חֲלוֹמֹתָיו״ — it appears that they were seeking a way to negate the matter entirely — perhaps they reasoned that by means of this degradation of selling him, Joseph is conclusively a slave, for the one who purchases him first — he is a slave to him, and it is impossible for him to leave his hand other than through another sale to someone else, and from now on he acquires the status of a permanent slave. And this is what it says (Psalms 105:17): ״לְעֶבֶד נִמְכַּר יוֹסֵף״ — and he is conclusively, fully determined, no longer able to change into a free man, and needless to say he cannot rise to greatness."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {4 "The verse citation in the Hebrew for comment 4 reads (Genesis 37:20) but the psalm citation is (Psalms 105:17); both rendered as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:47:02.696434391Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}